//? Title: Linear Algebra: Transformations, Systems and Projections
//? Subtitle: Hand-checkable matrix examples with datamunge::linalg, from basis vectors to conditioning
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a linear rule and some observations, we want to compute its output, recover its input,
or find the closest output it can produce. These are the matrix problems behind coordinate
changes, simultaneous equations and fitting a straight line. This tutorial starts with two
dimensions: a matrix sends a square to a parallelogram, a linear system finds the intersection
of two lines, and eigenvectors reveal directions that the matrix only stretches. A three-point
least-squares example then shows an orthogonal projection. Finally, a controlled perturbation
checks exactly how a condition number predicts sensitivity. All inputs are small constructed
examples; no external dataset is needed.
)

\toc

> The method: a matrix is a linear map

A vector \(x\in\mathbb R^n\) is a column of coordinates. A matrix
\(A\in\mathbb R^{m\times n}\) maps it to \(Ax\in\mathbb R^m\). Its columns
\(a_1,\ldots,a_n\) are the images of the standard basis vectors, so

$$
Ax=\sum_{j=1}^n x_j a_j,\qquad
A(\alpha x+\beta z)=\alpha Ax+\beta Az.
$$
\alttext(A times x is the sum of x j times column a j. A preserves linear combinations: A of alpha x plus beta z equals alpha A x plus beta A z.)

This gives a geometric reading of multiplication: scale each column by its input coordinate
and add. Matrix multiplication composes maps: \(BAx=B(Ax)\), so the rightmost map acts first.
The transpose \(A^\top\) exchanges rows and columns, and the dot product
\(x^\top z\) measures their alignment. The Euclidean length is
\(\lVert x\rVert_2=\sqrt{x^\top x}\); perpendicular vectors have zero dot product.

The diagram uses \(A=\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix}\). Its columns are independent,
so every output has a unique input. Its determinant is \(2\cdot2-1\cdot1=3\): areas grow
by a factor of three and orientation is preserved.

The concept diagram below is drawn by `dm::graphics::Diagram`. Its editable C++
source is the complete block below; regeneration uses the same datamunge toolchain
as the numerical examples. Exports consume the saved local SVG.

```{cpp, file="figures/linear_algebra_basis.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram;
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke arrow;
  arrow.arrow = true;
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke blue_line;
  blue_line.color = {37, 99, 235};
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke red_line;
  red_line.color = {220, 38, 38};
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke grey_line;
  grey_line.color = {148, 163, 184};
  auto dashed = grey_line;
  dashed.dashed = true;
  auto blue_arrow = blue_line;
  blue_arrow.arrow = true;
  auto orange_arrow = red_line;
  orange_arrow.arrow = true;
  orange_arrow.color = {234, 88, 12};
diagram.text({1.2, 3.8}, "Input coordinates")
      .text({8, 3.8}, "Output coordinates")
      .polygon({{0, 0}, {1, 0}, {1, 1}, {0, 1}}, {219, 234, 254, .6}, dashed)
      .polygon({{6.5, 0}, {8.5, 1}, {9.5, 3}, {7.5, 2}}, {219, 234, 254, .6}, dashed)
      .path({{-.3, 0}, {2.7, 0}}, arrow).path({{0, -.3}, {0, 3.1}}, arrow)
      .path({{6.2, 0}, {10, 0}}, arrow).path({{6.5, -.3}, {6.5, 3.3}}, arrow)
      .path({{0, 0}, {1, 0}}, blue_arrow).path({{0, 0}, {0, 1}}, orange_arrow)
      .path({{6.5, 0}, {8.5, 1}}, blue_arrow).path({{6.5, 0}, {7.5, 2}}, orange_arrow)
