```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/linear_algebra_basis.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The unit square maps to the parallelogram spanned by the columns of A. The image of its opposite corner is the sum of those columns.)
\alttext(On the left, blue e 1 and orange e 2 span a unit square. On the right their images, blue (2,1) and orange (1,2), span a parallelogram with opposite corner (3,3). The map multiplies area by three.)

The *column space* contains all possible outputs. Its dimension is the *rank*. The *null
space* contains inputs sent to zero. Rank plus null-space dimension equals \(n\): independent
columns give independent input directions, and every remaining direction can be combined
with them to make a null vector. Thus a square full-rank matrix has a unique solution to
\(Ax=b\). A rank-deficient matrix can have no solution or infinitely many, depending on
whether \(b\) lies in its column space.

For a square system, `dm::linalg::lu(A)` factors \(PA=LU\), where \(P\) permutes rows,
\(L\) is lower triangular and \(U\) is upper triangular. Solving \(Lz=Pb\) and then
\(Ux=z\) recovers the input. Partial pivoting chooses the largest available absolute entry
in each pivot column. Factorization takes \(O(n^3)\) operations; each further right-hand side
takes \(O(n^2)\). Factor once and reuse `.solve()` for several observations.

All C++ blocks below are independent complete programs using `<dm/dm.hpp>`. `DenseMatrix`
initialisers list entries row by row, and element indices start at zero. Program loops only
prepare plot coordinates or display results; the library performs the factorizations.

> Simple examples

>> Recovering the input: two intersecting equations

Take \(b=(4,5)^\top\). The rows of \(Ax=b\) give two lines. Elimination exposes the
intermediate equation and the answer:

$$
\begin{aligned}
2x_1+x_2&=4,\\
x_1+2x_2&=5,\\
\text{row 2}-\tfrac12\text{row 1}:\quad \tfrac32x_2&=3,
\qquad x_2=2,\quad x_1=1.
\end{aligned}
$$
\alttext(The equations are two x 1 plus x 2 equals four, and x 1 plus two x 2 equals five. Subtracting half of the first row from the second gives three halves x 2 equals three. Hence x 2 is two and x 1 is one.)

Equivalently, the output is the column combination
\(b=1(2,1)^\top+2(1,2)^\top\). The program solves the system and checks the result by
multiplying back. Forming the inverse would add work when only a solution is needed.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/linear_algebra_system.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> A(2, 2, {2, 1, 1, 2});
  const std::vector<double> b{4, 5};
  const auto factor = dm::linalg::lu(A);
  const auto x = factor.solve(b);
  const auto reconstructed = A * x;
  std::print("| quantity | first entry | second entry |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| recovered x | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n", x[0], x[1]);
  std::print("| A x | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n", reconstructed[0], reconstructed[1]);
  std::print("\ndeterminant = {:.0f}; backward error = {:.2e}\n",
             factor.det(), dm::linalg::backward_error(A, x, b));
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .line({-0.5, 2.5}, {5, -1}, "2 x1 + x2 = 4", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .line({-0.5, 2.5}, {2.75, 1.25}, "x1 + 2 x2 = 5", dm::RGB{234, 88, 12}, 2)
      .points({x[0]}, {x[1]}, "solution", dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}, 8)
      .text(1.12, 2.25, "(1, 2)")
      .x_limits(-0.6, 2.6).y_limits(-1.4, 5.5)
      .x_label("x1").y_label("x2").title("Solving A x = b")
      .size(760, 500).save_svg("build/linear_algebra_system.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| quantity | first entry | second entry |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| recovered x | 1.000000 | 2.000000 |
| A x | 4.000000 | 5.000000 |

determinant = 3; backward error = 0.00e+00
\image(figures/linear_algebra_system.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The recovered input and its image, followed by the two equations intersecting at (1,2). Backward error measures the residual relative to the sizes of A, x and b.)
\alttext(A descending blue line and a shallower orange line intersect at the green point (1,2). The table reports recovered input (1,2) and reconstructed output (4,5).)

>> Eigenvectors: directions that do not turn

An eigenvector \(v\ne0\) satisfies \(Av=\lambda v\). For this symmetric matrix the
sum and difference of the two coordinate directions give

$$
v_+=\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix},\quad Av_+=3v_+,
\qquad
v_-=\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1\\-1\end{pmatrix},\quad Av_-=v_-.
$$
\alttext(The unit vector in direction (1,1) has eigenvalue three. The perpendicular unit vector in direction (1,minus one) has eigenvalue one.)

Since these vectors are orthonormal, every vector has a unique decomposition
\(x=c_+v_++c_-v_-\), where \(c_\pm=v_\pm^\top x\). Applying the map gives
\(Ax=3c_+v_++c_-v_-\): resolve along the two axes, stretch, and recombine.
In matrix notation this is \(A=V\Lambda V^\top\). A unit circle therefore becomes an
ellipse with semiaxes 3 and 1. Their product is the area multiplier, matching the determinant.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/linear_algebra_eigen.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <numbers>

int main() {
  using Matrix = dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double>;
  const Matrix A(2, 2, {2, 1, 1, 2});
  const auto eigen = dm::linalg::symmetric_eigen(A);
  Matrix diagonal(2, 2);
  std::print("| eigenvalue | v1 | v2 | norm of A v - lambda v |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < 2; ++j) {
    diagonal(j, j) = eigen.eigenvalues[j];
    const auto v = eigen.eigenvectors.col(j);
    const auto residual = A * Matrix::from_columns({v}) - Matrix::from_columns({v}) * diagonal(j, j);
    std::print("| {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.2e} |\n",
               diagonal(j, j), v[0], v[1], residual.norm_frobenius());
  }
  const auto& V = eigen.eigenvectors;
  std::print("\nreconstruction error = {:.2e}; orthogonality error = {:.2e}\n",
             (A - V * diagonal * V.transpose()).norm_frobenius(),
             (V.transpose() * V - Matrix::identity(2)).norm_frobenius());
  Matrix circle(2, 129);
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < circle.cols(); ++i) {
    const double angle = 2 * std::numbers::pi * static_cast<double>(i) / 128;
    circle(0, i) = std::cos(angle);
    circle(1, i) = std::sin(angle);
  }
  const auto ellipse = A * circle;
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(circle.row(0), circle.row(1), "unit circle", dm::RGB{100, 116, 139}, 2)
      .line(ellipse.row(0), ellipse.row(1), "image under A", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2);
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < 2; ++j) {
    const auto v = V.col(j);
    const double length = diagonal(j, j);
    plot.segments({-length * v[0]}, {-length * v[1]},
                  {length * v[0]}, {length * v[1]}, dm::RGB{234, 88, 12}, 2);
  }
  plot.x_limits(-3.5, 3.5).y_limits(-3.5, 3.5)
      .x_label("first coordinate").y_label("second coordinate")
      .title("Eigenvectors are the ellipse axes")
      .size(660, 680).margins(70, 30, 55, 65)
      .save_svg("build/linear_algebra_eigen.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| eigenvalue | v1 | v2 | norm of A v - lambda v |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 3.000000 | 0.707107 | 0.707107 | 0.00e+00 |
| 1.000000 | 0.707107 | -0.707107 | 1.57e-16 |

reconstruction error = 4.44e-16; orthogonality error = 3.14e-16
\image(figures/linear_algebra_eigen.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The eigensolver returns unit eigenvectors as columns, ordered by decreasing eigenvalue. The plot uses equal axis scales: orange diameters show the stretched eigenvector directions.)
\alttext(A grey unit circle becomes a blue ellipse tilted at 45 degrees. Its orange principal semiaxes have lengths three along (1,1) and one along (1,minus one).)

Eigenvector signs are arbitrary: \(-v\) has the same eigenvalue as \(v\). The residual and
reconstruction checks avoid depending on a particular sign convention. The real orthogonal
decomposition used here applies to symmetric matrices; general real matrices need not have
a real eigenbasis. Singular values describe stretching even in that general case.

>> Least squares: the closest attainable output

Fit a line \(y\approx\beta_0+\beta_1t\) through the three points
\((0,1),(1,2),(2,4)\). There are more equations than unknowns, and no line passes through
all three. The columns of the design matrix span a plane in three-dimensional observation
space. Least squares projects \(y\) onto that plane:

$$
X=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\1&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix},\qquad
y=\begin{pmatrix}1\\2\\4\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\widehat\beta=\arg\min_\beta\lVert y-X\beta\rVert_2^2.
$$
\alttext(X has rows (1,0), (1,1), and (1,2). The response is (1,2,4). Choose beta to minimise the squared length of y minus X beta.)

Differentiate the squared length: its gradient is \(-2X^\top(y-X\beta)\). At a minimum
\(X^\top r=0\), where \(r=y-X\widehat\beta\). The residual is perpendicular to every
column of \(X\). Here the normal equations and their solution are

$$
\begin{pmatrix}3&3\\3&5\end{pmatrix}\widehat\beta
=\begin{pmatrix}7\\10\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\widehat\beta=\begin{pmatrix}5/6\\3/2\end{pmatrix},\qquad
r=\begin{pmatrix}1/6\\-1/3\\1/6\end{pmatrix},\quad \lVert r\rVert_2^2=1/6.
$$
\alttext(The normal equations have matrix rows (3,3) and (3,5), with right-hand side (7,10). The intercept is five sixths and the slope is three halves. The residual is (one sixth, minus one third, one sixth), with squared length one sixth.)

Orthogonality also proves optimality: for any coefficient change \(h\),
\(\lVert r-Xh\rVert_2^2=\lVert r\rVert_2^2+\lVert Xh\rVert_2^2\).
It proves uniqueness when the columns of \(X\) are independent. In particular, the two
checks here are \(\sum r_i=0\) and \(\sum t_i r_i=0\).

The program uses the default Householder QR solver. With \(X=QR\), orthogonality of
\(Q\) preserves distances; a triangular solve recovers the coefficients without forming
\(X^\top X\). This matters because forming the normal equations squares the 2-norm
condition number. If columns are dependent, QR rejects the design; select
`dm::linalg::LeastSquaresMethod::SVD` to obtain a minimum-norm solution instead.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/linear_algebra_projection.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const std::vector<double> t{0, 1, 2}, y{1, 2, 4};
  const dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> X(3, 2, {1, 0, 1, 1, 1, 2});
  const auto fit = dm::linalg::least_squares(X, y);
  const auto fitted = X * fit.coefficients;
  const auto orthogonality = X.transpose() * fit.residuals;
  std::print("intercept = {:.6f}; slope = {:.6f}; rank = {}\n\n",
             fit.coefficients[0], fit.coefficients[1], fit.rank);
  std::print("| t | observed | fitted | residual |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.size(); ++i)
    std::print("| {:.0f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n", t[i], y[i], fitted[i], fit.residuals[i]);
  std::print("\nsquared residual norm = {:.6f}; X^T r = ({:.2e}, {:.2e})\n",
             fit.residual_norm * fit.residual_norm, orthogonality[0], orthogonality[1]);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .line(t, fitted, "least-squares line", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .segments(t, fitted, t, y, dm::RGB{234, 88, 12}, 3)
      .points(t, y, "observed", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 7)
      .points(t, fitted, "fitted", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 5)
      .x_limits(-0.3, 2.4).y_limits(0, 5.3)
      .x_label("t").y_label("y").title("Least-squares line and residuals")
      .size(760, 500).save_svg("build/linear_algebra_projection.svg");
}
```
