\image(figures/linear_algebra_projection.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The coefficients and residuals match the fractions above. Orange vertical segments show observation errors; orthogonality holds in the three-dimensional response space, as checked by X transpose r, rather than between these segments and the drawn line.)
\alttext(Three observed points at (0,1), (1,2), and (2,4), with a blue fitted line of intercept five sixths and slope three halves. Vertical residuals point up, down, and up; their sum and their t-weighted sum are zero to rounding precision.)

> Checking the theory: sensitivity is a property of the problem

A small residual does not by itself guarantee an accurate input. For nonsingular \(A\),
perturbing only the right-hand side gives \(A\delta x=\delta b\), hence

$$
\frac{\lVert\delta x\rVert_2}{\lVert x\rVert_2}
\le\underbrace{\lVert A\rVert_2\lVert A^{-1}\rVert_2}_{\kappa_2(A)}
\frac{\lVert\delta b\rVert_2}{\lVert b\rVert_2},\qquad
\kappa_2(A)=\frac{\sigma_{\max}(A)}{\sigma_{\min}(A)}.
$$
\alttext(The relative change in x is at most the condition number of A times the relative change in b. The 2-norm condition number is the product of the norms of A and its inverse, or the largest singular value divided by the smallest.)

The bound follows from \(\lVert\delta x\rVert\le\lVert A^{-1}\rVert\lVert\delta b\rVert\)
and \(\lVert b\rVert\le\lVert A\rVert\lVert x\rVert\). To attain it, use
\(A_\tau=\operatorname{diag}(1,\tau)\), \(b=(1,0)^\top\) and
\(\delta b=(0,10^{-8})^\top\), for \(0<\tau\le1\). Then
\(x=(1,0)^\top\), \(\delta x=(0,10^{-8}/\tau)^\top\), and
\(\kappa_2(A_\tau)=1/\tau\). We change the input deliberately; this experiment measures
data sensitivity, not floating-point error introduced by the solver.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/linear_algebra_conditioning.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const std::vector<double> b{1, 0}, perturbed_b{1, 1e-8};
  std::vector<double> log_condition, log_change;
  std::print("| tau | condition number | relative input change | relative solution change | bound |\n");
  std::print("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (double tau : {1.0, 1e-2, 1e-4, 1e-6, 1e-8}) {
    const dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> A(2, 2, {1, 0, 0, tau});
    const auto factor = dm::linalg::lu(A);
    const auto x = factor.solve(b), perturbed_x = factor.solve(perturbed_b);
    const double condition = dm::linalg::condition_number(A);
    const double input_change = dm::linalg::relative_error(perturbed_b, b);
    const double solution_change = dm::linalg::relative_error(perturbed_x, x);
    std::print("| {:.0e} | {:.0e} | {:.0e} | {:.0e} | {:.0e} |\n",
               tau, condition, input_change, solution_change, condition * input_change);
    log_condition.push_back(std::log10(condition));
    log_change.push_back(std::log10(solution_change));
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .line(log_condition, log_change, "solution change", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .points(log_condition, log_change, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 6)
      .line({0, 8}, {-8, -8}, "input change", dm::RGB{234, 88, 12}, 2)
      .x_ticks({0, 2, 4, 6, 8}, {"1", "100", "1e4", "1e6", "1e8"})
      .y_ticks({-8, -6, -4, -2, 0}, {"1e-8", "1e-6", "1e-4", "0.01", "1"})
      .x_limits(-0.3, 8.4).y_limits(-8.8, 2)
      .x_label("condition number (log scale)").y_label("relative change (log scale)")
      .title("The condition-number bound is attained")
      .size(800, 520).save_svg("build/linear_algebra_conditioning.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| tau | condition number | relative input change | relative solution change | bound |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1e+00 | 1e+00 | 1e-08 | 1e-08 | 1e-08 |
| 1e-02 | 1e+02 | 1e-08 | 1e-06 | 1e-06 |
| 1e-04 | 1e+04 | 1e-08 | 1e-04 | 1e-04 |
| 1e-06 | 1e+06 | 1e-08 | 1e-02 | 1e-02 |
| 1e-08 | 1e+08 | 1e-08 | 1e+00 | 1e+00 |
\image(figures/linear_algebra_conditioning.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Five generated diagonal systems attain the perturbation bound. The relative change in b stays at one part in a hundred million, while the relative change in x grows to one.)
\alttext(A log-log plot shows a horizontal orange input-change line at ten to the minus eight and a blue solution-change line rising with slope one, reaching one at condition number ten to the eight.)

The final system is nonsingular, but its weak second direction magnifies the input change
by \(10^8\). At \(\tau=0\) it loses rank: the second input coordinate becomes invisible,
and the perturbed observation cannot be matched at all. This is why numerical rank decisions
and measurement precision matter as well as the solver's residual.

> Conclusion

Columns explain a transformation; rows explain its simultaneous equations. Eigenvectors
expose invariant directions, and least squares finds the closest output when observations
cannot be matched exactly. LU, the symmetric eigensolver and Householder QR reproduce the
small examples above, with residual and reconstruction checks. The diagonal sweep attains
the condition-number bound and shows how an accurate solve can still be sensitive to data.
These examples concern small real dense matrices. For factorization derivations, rank-deficient
fits and dataset applications, continue with the dense linear algebra report at
`../numerics/linear_algebra/dense_linear_algebra.mepml`.
